# Environment for the haaku API. One template for both supported ways to run
# it — the container install (docker-compose.yml / docker-compose.advanced.yml)
# and a source run on the host (`make dev-backend`):
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   # set HAAKU_SECRET_KEY, TVDB_API_KEY and TMDB_API_TOKEN below; on the first
#   # start of an empty local-mode instance also set
#   # HAAKU_ADMIN_EMAIL / HAAKU_ADMIN_PASSWORD
#   # then either:
#   docker compose up -d              # container install
#   make dev-db && make dev-backend   # source run (Vite: make dev-frontend)
#
# .env is gitignored; NEVER commit real values.

# --- Required -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Root key for everything stored encrypted at rest. The server refuses to start
# without it, and it must be at least 32 characters. Generate one:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Back it up SEPARATELY from the config directory: a database dump full of
# ciphertext that travels with the key gives no confidentiality, and losing the
# key makes the encrypted secrets unrecoverable.
HAAKU_SECRET_KEY=

# TheTVDB v4 API key (https://thetvdb.com/dashboard/account/apikey). TVDB is
# haaku's PRIMARY media source: the tvdb id is the media identity, and titles,
# overviews, genres, seasons, episodes and artwork are ingested from it. Without
# this key the catalogue cannot be populated and the server refuses to start.
TVDB_API_KEY=

# Subscriber PIN, only for a TVDB project that requires one. Usually empty.
TVDB_PIN=

# TMDB v4 read access token (https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api). TMDB is
# SUPPLEMENTARY: it supplies the tmdb/imdb link, ratings, popularity, the tagline
# and a poster fallback for the titles TVDB has no artwork for. Server-side only;
# never expose it to browsers.
TMDB_API_TOKEN=

# --- Owner account (local mode) ----------------------------------------------
#
# The owner account is created from these two values on the first start of an
# empty instance, before the API answers anything. It is the admin account you
# log in with; there is no "whoever signs up first becomes the owner" any more.
#
# Both are REQUIRED on the first start of a local-mode instance: with the
# database empty and these unset, the server refuses to start (rather than come
# up with nobody able to administer it). After the owner exists they are not
# consulted again, and a second start never modifies the account — so you can
# leave them set or blank them out once you are signed in.
#
# The password must be at least 10 characters. It is hashed with Argon2id and
# never logged.
#
# These are ignored entirely in clerk mode (HAAKU_AUTH_MODE=clerk).
HAAKU_ADMIN_EMAIL=
HAAKU_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- Data layer ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Leave DATABASE_URL and REDIS_URL unset and the container install runs its own
# PostgreSQL and Redis. Setting one switches the matching bundled service off —
# that is what docker-compose.advanced.yml does, naming the containers next to
# the app. A source run needs DATABASE_URL pointing at the host's PostgreSQL
# (`make dev-db` publishes 55432, `make pg-up` prints a unix-socket URL).
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://haaku:haaku@postgres:5432/haaku?sslmode=disable
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://haaku:<password>@localhost:55432/haaku?sslmode=disable
# REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:56379
#
# For a source run against `make dev-db`, put those host URLs in the gitignored
# `.env.local` instead (sourced after this file by `make dev-backend`, so it
# wins). Keep them out of `.env`: the omnibus container reads that file too, and
# a non-empty DATABASE_URL or REDIS_URL makes it skip its bundled service.

# Password for the postgres service in docker-compose.advanced.yml.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=haaku

# --- Identity -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# local (default) issues identity in this server: sign-up, login, sessions and
# passwords hashed with Argon2id. clerk delegates identity to Clerk and
# registers no local auth routes. The owner above is seeded only in local mode.
HAAKU_AUTH_MODE=local

# Local sign-up policy, only consulted in local mode:
#   approval (default) — every new sign-up is created pending and cannot log in
#                        until an admin approves it.
#   open              — new accounts are active immediately.
#   closed            — no sign-ups at all (the owner already exists).
HAAKU_SIGNUP=approval

# Admin/mod whitelists for Clerk mode only (comma-separated Clerk user IDs).
# ADMIN_USER_IDS=
# MOD_USER_IDS=

# Clerk is optional and only used when HAAKU_AUTH_MODE=clerk. The dev stack in
# dev/compose.yml runs a clerk-emulator on :4000; leave these unset for local.
# CLERK_API_BASE_URL=
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY=
# CLERK_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Object storage (S3 / MinIO) ----------------------------------------------
#
# Used for list covers and imports. dev/compose.yml runs MinIO on :9000.
# S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
# S3_ACCESS_KEY=haaku
# S3_SECRET_KEY=<minio-secret>

# --- Container install --------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything in this section only matters when running the published image. A
# source run ignores it and uses PORT/APP_ENV below.

# Host port for the web UI. The container always listens on 8080.
HAAKU_PORT=8080

# Host directory holding the database, the Redis append-only file, the app's
# own directory and the pre-migration backups. Back this up.
HAAKU_CONFIG=./config

# The exact image to run. Pin a digest for a reproducible install:
#   HAAKU_IMAGE=ghcr.io/haaku-tv/haaku@sha256:<digest>
# HAAKU_IMAGE=ghcr.io/haaku-tv/haaku:latest

# The account the app runs as inside the container. Set these when the config
# directory is a bind mount owned by a host user, so the app can write to it.
# PUID=911
# PGID=911

# TZ=UTC

# --- Source runs --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PORT and APP_ENV are read directly by the API process. The image sets
# APP_ENV=production itself, so leave these commented for a container install:
# setting APP_ENV=development there would register dev-only routes.
#
# PORT=8080
# APP_ENV=development

# --- Background workers -------------------------------------------------------
#
# The app runs its background workers (webhook delivery, activity fan-out, the
# event outbox, the TMDB maintenance jobs) inside the API process, so one
# container is still the whole install. Leave HAAKU_WORKER unset for that.
#
# To run the workers in a process of their own instead, set
# HAAKU_WORKER_SERVICE=true: the init then starts a second service in this
# container and turns the API's own workers off, so each worker runs in exactly
# one place. Optionally restrict that service to a subset:
# HAAKU_WORKER_SERVICE=true
# HAAKU_WORKER_SERVICE_SELECTION=webhooks,activity,outbox,metadata,stats
#
# The worker process answers /healthz and /readyz on its own address (the API
# answers on the app port, so a worker needs one that does not collide):
# HAAKU_WORKER_HEALTH_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:9090
#
# In the multi-container layout (docker-compose.advanced.yml) every service
# reads this file, so HAAKU_WORKER=none is what leaves the workers to the
# `worker` service, and HAAKU_WORKER_SELECTION picks that service's subset:
# HAAKU_WORKER=none
# HAAKU_WORKER_SELECTION=all
#
# A source run hands the workers to a separate process the same way:
#   HAAKU_WORKER=none  go run ./cmd/api
#   HAAKU_MODE=worker  HAAKU_WORKER=all  go run ./cmd/api
#
# TMDB maintenance (the metadata worker): where the change-feed cursor lives and
# how many popular pages one backfill pass walks.
# METADATA_SINCE_FILE=.hermes/metadata_sync_since.txt
# METADATA_BACKFILL_PAGES=5

# --- Optional tuning ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=20
# DB_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=5
# DB_CONN_MAX_LIFETIME_MINUTES=30
# DB_SLOW_QUERY_THRESHOLD_MS=10
# CORS_ORIGINS=

# --- Anti-abuse limits --------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every request is metered by endpoint GROUP (read, media, sync, write, auth,
# admin — see internal/ratelimit/groups.go) over two ceilings: a burst window
# and a daily request budget. The per-group numbers are a table in Go, not env
# vars, so there is nothing to mis-tune here; these are the deployment knobs.
# Full description, including the response headers and the failure mode:
# internal/ratelimit/ratelimit.md.
#
# ON by default, and both layers FAIL OPEN if Redis is unavailable, so a box
# without Redis still serves. Set false for local development.
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
#
# The burst window every group's ceiling is measured over, in seconds.
# RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
#
# Requests that came straight from this machine (loopback peer, no forwarded
# header: your own shell, the container's health check, a dev server that
# forwards without headers) skip the limiter entirely. Set false if this
# instance is reachable by other people and you want even localhost metered.
# RATE_LIMIT_EXEMPT_LOCAL=true
#
# Which proxies may set the client address. A request's X-Forwarded-For /
# X-Real-IP is believed ONLY when the connection comes from one of these
# networks (loopback is always trusted). This is what makes anonymous traffic
# meter per household member instead of per proxy: without it, every client
# behind your reverse proxy shares one bucket keyed to the proxy's own address.
# Declare the network your proxy runs in, e.g. a docker bridge:
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=172.18.0.0/16,10.0.0.5

